blackbox: x86: automatic ramoops region

ramoops needs a fixed physical region given by memmap= and ramoops.*
parameters. A hard-coded address is hardware specific and becomes unsafe
after a RAM or firmware change: it may end up in ACPI or firmware memory.

Add "bbox_ramoops=auto": during early boot (early_param, after the e820
map is set up and before memblock) pick a 1 MiB region by a fixed rule:
the largest usable RAM range, 256 MiB from its start, 2 MiB aligned.
Skip it if it overlaps the kernel image (KASLR), the initrd or setup_data,
shifting by 64 MiB up to 8 times. Reserve it in e820 like memmap=nn$ss and
register a "ramoops" platform device for it (1 MiB, 128 KiB records,
256 KiB blackbox zone, ECC).

The same memory map gives the same address on every boot, so records
survive a warm reboot. After a hardware or firmware change the region
moves into the new usable RAM: only the record made before the change is
lost. Explicit ramoops.mem_address= takes precedence.

Requires 0008 and 0009.

Tested in QEMU (q35, NVMe GPT disk): the same region on every boot,
reboot and "echo c > /proc/sysrq-trigger" produce logs in /data/log/bbox
with events UNKNOWN and PANIC.

diff --git a/drivers/staging/blackbox/x86/Kconfig b/drivers/staging/blackbox/x86/Kconfig
index f23c465fc..0216ec43e 100644
--- a/drivers/staging/blackbox/x86/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/staging/blackbox/x86/Kconfig
@@ -9,3 +9,13 @@ config BLACKBOX_X86
 	  System adapter for BlackBox on x86 (based on the rockchip one).
 	  Catches panic via panic_notifier and saves fault logs with
 	  pstore ram (ramoops).
+
+config BLACKBOX_X86_RAMOOPS_AUTO
+	bool "Automatic ramoops region for BlackBox on x86"
+	depends on BLACKBOX_X86 && PSTORE_RAM
+	default y
+	help
+	  With "bbox_ramoops=auto" on the kernel command line, pick a ramoops
+	  region during early boot by a fixed rule from the firmware memory
+	  map and register a ramoops device for it. No hardware-specific
+	  memmap=/ramoops.* parameters are needed.
